DIET Classifier and TED Policy in Rasa – 3-Minute Speech
Today I will explain DIET Classifier and TED Policy in Rasa.
These are the two main components that make a Rasa chatbot intelligent and conversational.
DIET Classifier – Understanding the User
DIET stands for Dual Intent and Entity Transformer.
The DIET Classifier is responsible for understanding the user message.
It does two tasks at the same time:
Intent classification, meaning what the user wants
Entity extraction, meaning important values like platform name or environment
DIET is based on a Transformer architecture, which is the same core idea used in models like BERT.
Because of this, DIET understands the context and meaning of a sentence instead of only matching keywords.
For example, when a user says:
“I want to onboard to the Malts platform in the UAT environment”
DIET understands:
Intent: onboard_platform
Entities: platform = Malts, environment = UAT
Since DIET uses embeddings and transformer layers, it works well even with less training data, gives high accuracy, and keeps the NLU pipeline simple and fast.
In simple words, DIET is the brain that understands what the user is saying.
TED Policy – Deciding the Response
TED stands for Transformer Embedding Dialogue policy.
While DIET understands the message, TED decides what the chatbot should do next.
TED is also based on Transformer models, similar to BERT-like architectures, but it is used for conversation flow, not language understanding.
TED looks at:
Current user intent
Extracted entities
Conversation history
Stored information called slots
Previous bot actions
Using transformer-based embeddings, TED understands the full conversation context, not just the last message.
For example:
User: “I want to onboard to the Malts platform”
Bot: “Which environment do you want to onboard to?”
User: “UAT”
TED remembers the context and correctly decides the next response or action, such as
starting the onboarding process for Malts in UAT.
Because of this, TED handles multi-step and complex conversations smoothly and reduces the need for hard-coded rules.
In simple words, TED is the brain that decides the next response.
Final Summary
To summarize:
DIET Classifier uses Transformer and BERT-like concepts to understand user intent and entities
TED Policy uses Transformer-based embeddings to manage conversation flow
DIET understands what the user says
TED decides what the bot should say next
Together, DIET and TED make the Rasa chatbot smart, scalable, and human-like.
